## 8. Market scan (evidence, not recommendation)

All URLs verified live 3 August 2026. Vendor-published figures are marked as such; treat them as marketing until independently verified.

**The consolidation wave reshaped the landscape in 12 months:**
- Workday completed its acquisition of Paradox (conversational high-volume hiring AI, "Olivia") on 1 October 2025; Paradox is now Workday's candidate experience agent alongside HiredScore screening. [Workday newsroom](https://newsroom.workday.com/2025-10-01-Workday-Completes-Acquisition-of-Paradox), [Bersin's analysis](https://joshbersin.com/2025/08/workday-to-acquire-paradox-a-bigger-deal-than-you-think/), [Workday TA stack](https://blog.workday.com/en-us/workday-leads-talent-acquisition-agentic-era.html)
- SAP acquired SmartRecruiters (announced 1 August 2025, closed Q4 2025) explicitly to fill SuccessFactors' high-volume recruiting gap. [erp.today](https://erp.today/sap-announces-acquisition-of-smartrecruiters/), [Unleash interview](https://www.unleash.ai/talent-acquisition/inside-saps-acquisition-of-smartrecruiters-with-ceo-rebecca-carr/)
- Humanforce absorbed Australian high-volume player LiveHire and delisted it from the ASX (March 2025).
- 2026 Gartner MQ for TA Suites Leaders per vendor self-reporting: Workday, Oracle, Avature, iCIMS; Eightfold and Phenom as Visionaries. Placements are reconstructed from vendor announcements (the MQ itself is paywalled), so treat as a floor, not the full grid.

**Implication for VA:** whichever HRIS VA runs (to confirm in week 1) now largely determines the gravitational pull of the replatform option, because the conversational high-volume layer has been absorbed into the suites.

**High-volume assessment layer:**
- Sapia.ai (Melbourne-founded): blind chat-based structured interviews. Verified Kmart Group case study: 12,000 hires/year, time-to-hire 44 days to 11.8 days, vendor-published. [Kmart case study](https://sapia.ai/resources/blog/how-kmart-group-used-ai-interviews-to-fix-high-volume-hiring/)
- HireVue: incumbent global video assessment; discontinued facial analysis in 2021 after an FTC complaint and an audit finding it contributed little predictive validity. A cautionary tale on assessment-AI claims. [EPIC](https://epic.org/hirevue-facing-ftc-complaint-from-epic-halts-use-of-facial-recognition/), [HireVue](https://www.hirevue.com/)

**Airline deployments, with evidence quality attached:**
- **Qantas on Sapia.ai** (cabin crew, ground crew, graduates) is the only well-documented airline AI hiring deployment found, and every figure is vendor-published with internally inconsistent units and no independent audit. Useful as an existence proof, not as a benchmark. [Sapia Qantas page](https://sapia.ai/customer-stories/qantas/)
- **Delta** uses HireVue for flight-attendant interviews; whether algorithmic scoring or just async video with human review is undocumented, and that distinction carries the legal weight. [Delta careers](https://www.delta.com/us/en/careers/flight-attendant-careers)
- **United** reportedly uses Phenom high-volume automation for flight-attendant hiring (vendor-sourced). [Phenom blog](https://www.phenom.com/blog/redefining-talent-acquisition-united-airlines-better-hiring)
- **British Airways** discloses no AI interviewing; **Emirates** AI-screening claims trace only to content farms. Do not repeat them. [BA hiring process](https://careers.ba.com/hiring-process)

**Regulatory and liability context for any AI assessment step:**
- **Australia first:** DISR's proposed mandatory guardrails for high-risk AI explicitly name employment and recruitment as a high-risk setting, with the Voluntary AI Safety Standard (September 2024) as the interim reference. Privacy Act reforms introduce automated-decision-making transparency obligations, and AHRC guidance addresses algorithmic bias in hiring. Together these define what an AI screening step must be able to demonstrate.
- Mobley v. Workday established that AI hiring vendors can be directly liable as employer agents (US precedent; the reasoning travels).
- EU AI Act high-risk hiring obligations slipped from August 2026 to December 2027 under the May 2026 Digital Omnibus; the direction is unchanged and Annex III remains the de facto global benchmark for hiring AI.
- Fosway's research finding that only 27% of vendor-claimed AI features are live with customers is the right prior for every vendor conversation.

A governance and legal-exposure section is a required part of the Phase 3 options paper, not an appendix. It is the single most common omission that gets hiring-AI proposals killed at CIO level.

## 9. Open questions for Nick

1. **Mandate:** is this discovery formally sanctioned by Tom, or are you building the case to sanction it? The stakeholder sequence changes accordingly.
2. **The BCG report:** can you get the underlying model (data request item 11)? Reconciling their number is the single highest-leverage analysis in the pack.
3. **HRIS:** which HRIS does VA run? This is the biggest known unknown in the replatform fork.
4. **Timebox:** is 6 weeks the right shape for your credibility clock, or does Tom need a first readout sooner?
5. **Scope edge:** is internal mobility / redeployment in scope, or external hiring only?
6. **The savings word:** do you know yet whether leadership hears "savings" as headcount or capacity? Worth knowing before the first TA interview, because it determines how guarded people will be.
7. **Audience:** is this pack for Tom as a working document, or genuinely headed for CIO/CHRO? Consulting-grade polish for an audience of one reads as a new hire overreaching; confirm before optimising the artefacts.

## 10. Method note (reusable)

The pattern used here, for the next BCG line item: (1) classify the claim's constraints as hard, soft, or assumption; (2) find the structure generating the symptom, not the symptom; (3) state falsifiable hypotheses; (4) design questions and data collection against the hypotheses; (5) let the baseline decide sequencing; (6) verify every external claim to its primary source and mark vendor figures as vendor figures.
